Here is a dynamic programming version rather than the usual pointer scan. After sorting, `dp[i][j]` stores the best number of satisfied students using the first `i` students and first `j` cookies.

At each pair, the current cookie can be skipped, or it can satisfy the current student when its size is large enough. The larger of those choices is kept. This is more memory-heavy, but the table makes the two possible decisions explicit.

```javascript
class Solution {
    findMaximumCookieStudents(Student, Cookie) {
        Student.sort((a, b) => a - b);
        Cookie.sort((a, b) => a - b);

        const studentCount = Student.length;
        const cookieCount = Cookie.length;
        const dp = Array.from(
            { length: studentCount + 1 },
            () => Array(cookieCount + 1).fill(0)
        );

        for (let i = 1; i <= studentCount; i++) {
            for (let j = 1; j <= cookieCount; j++) {
                dp[i][j] = dp[i][j - 1];

                if (Student[i - 1] <= Cookie[j - 1]) {
                    dp[i][j] = Math.max(
                        dp[i][j],
                        dp[i - 1][j - 1] + 1
                    );
                }
            }
        }

        return dp[studentCount][cookieCount];
    }
}
```

For students `[1, 2]` and cookies `[1, 1, 3]`, the first cookie matches `1`, the second can be skipped, and cookie `3` matches `2`. The final table value is `2`.

This also handles either input being empty because the table still has a valid zero row or column. Sorting changes both input arrays.

Time complexity: `O(n log n + m log m + n * m)`.
Space complexity: `O(n * m)` for the table.

Sorting both lists makes the matching order much easier. I keep one pointer on the students and one on the cookies. The smallest student is checked first, so a cookie that is too small can safely be skipped. When a cookie is large enough, it is used and both pointers move forward.

The input lists are sorted in place.

```cpp
class Solution{    
    public:
    int findMaximumCookieStudents(vector<int>& Student, vector<int>& Cookie){
        sort(Student.begin(), Student.end());
        sort(Cookie.begin(), Cookie.end());

        int studentIndex = 0;
        int cookieIndex = 0;
        int satisfied = 0;

        while (studentIndex < Student.size() && cookieIndex < Cookie.size()) {
            if (Cookie[cookieIndex] >= Student[studentIndex]) {
                satisfied++;
                studentIndex++;
            }
            cookieIndex++;
        }

        return satisfied;
    }
};
```

For example, with students `[1, 2, 3]` and cookies `[1, 1, 2]`, the first cookie satisfies `1`, the second is too small for `2`, and the last cookie satisfies `2`. The result is `2`.

Time complexity: `O(n log n + m log m)`.
Space complexity: `O(1)` extra space, not counting the sorting internals.

Instead of moving through a sorted cookie array, this version stores cookie sizes in a `SortedDictionary`. For each student, it searches the dictionary from the smallest size and takes the first size that is large enough. The count attached to that size is reduced after use.

This keeps duplicate cookies together. The student list is sorted in place, but the cookie list itself is only read into the dictionary.

```csharp
public class Solution
{
    public int FindMaximumCookieStudents(List<int> Student, List<int> Cookie)
    {
        Student.Sort();

        var cookieCounts = new SortedDictionary<int, int>();
        foreach (int size in Cookie)
        {
            if (!cookieCounts.ContainsKey(size))
            {
                cookieCounts[size] = 0;
            }
            cookieCounts[size]++;
        }

        int satisfied = 0;

        foreach (int need in Student)
        {
            int selectedSize = -1;

            foreach (int size in cookieCounts.Keys)
            {
                if (size >= need)
                {
                    selectedSize = size;
                    break;
                }
            }

            if (selectedSize == -1)
            {
                continue;
            }

            satisfied++;
            cookieCounts[selectedSize]--;
            if (cookieCounts[selectedSize] == 0)
            {
                cookieCounts.Remove(selectedSize);
            }
        }

        return satisfied;
    }
}
```

If the cookie list is empty, the dictionary stays empty and no student can be matched. The dictionary also means duplicate sizes do not need separate entries.

Time complexity: `O(n log n + m log m + n * k)`, where `k` is the number of different cookie sizes checked during searches.
Space complexity: `O(k)` for the dictionary.

This version starts from the largest student and the largest cookie. If the cookie can satisfy that student, I count the match and move both indexes back. If it is too small, that student cannot be satisfied by any smaller cookie, so the student pointer moves alone.

Both arrays are changed because `Arrays.sort` is used. Empty input is also covered by the loop condition.

```java
class Solution {
    public int findMaximumCookieStudents(int[] Student, int[] Cookie) {
        Arrays.sort(Student);
        Arrays.sort(Cookie);

        int studentIndex = Student.length - 1;
        int cookieIndex = Cookie.length - 1;
        int satisfied = 0;

        while (studentIndex >= 0 && cookieIndex >= 0) {
            if (Cookie[cookieIndex] >= Student[studentIndex]) {
                satisfied++;
                cookieIndex--;
                studentIndex--;
            } else {
                studentIndex--;
            }
        }

        return satisfied;
    }
}
```

A useful edge case is when there are no cookies. `cookieIndex` starts below zero, so the method returns zero without reading the array.

Time complexity: `O(n log n + m log m)`.
Space complexity: `O(1)` apart from the space used internally by sorting.

## Solution
```java
class Solution {
    public int findMaximumCookieStudents(int[] student, int[] cookie) {
         // Sort both arrays to apply the greedy strategy
        Thread t1 = new Thread(() -> {Arrays.sort(student);});
        Thread t2 = new Thread(() -> {Arrays.sort(cookie);});

        t1.start();
        t2.start();

        try {
        t1.join();
        t2.join();
        }
        catch(InterruptedException e) {

        }

        int studentIndex = 0;
        int cookieIndex = 0;

        // Try to assign cookies until any one list is fully processed
        while (studentIndex < student.length && cookieIndex < cookie.length) {
            // If the cookie satisfies the student's greed
            if (cookie[cookieIndex] >= student[studentIndex]) {
                studentIndex++;
            }
            // Move to next cookie in both cases
            cookieIndex++;
        }

        // Number of students satisfied is equal to studentIndex
        return studentIndex;
    }
}

```

